how it scales out.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.030
You're growing.

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:49.470
Example is growing.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.810
You have more
departments coming in.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.990
You certainly have more
interest and more projects

00:10:54.990 --> 00:11:01.670
and it will grow both depth-wise
and horizontally as well.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:04.880
A quick checkpoint-- we know
what we're trying to achieve,

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:09.800
security, ease of administration
and minimal stress.

00:11:09.800 --> 00:11:13.050
You're in the broad
middle of the spectrum.

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.350
Your systems have
multiple users all with

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:17.030
different requirements.

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:20.660
You have multiple systems and
you're growing, potentially

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:22.735
growing rapidly.

00:11:22.735 --> 00:11:24.110
So what we're
going to do next is

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:27.620
we're going to overlay the
two concepts that we have.

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:29.780
We have a premised architecture.

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:30.485
We have Example.

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:35.200
Let's do this mentally
to start with.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:38.380
Now, first of all,
you have users.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:41.280
I'm going to second myself
into example for the purposes

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:42.490
of this presentation.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:45.040
You have Corrie at example.com.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:46.630
We have Bjergsen.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:47.920
We have Keane.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:49.884
We have Froggen. We have Impact.

00:11:49.884 --> 00:11:51.550
We have all these
people at example.com,

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:55.510
and those people play
that variety of roles.

00:11:55.510 --> 00:11:58.250
Some of them merely
want to view data.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:01.090
Some of them are analysts
that are doing our analysis

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:02.510
of machine learning.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:04.360
Some of them are
administrators that

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:06.480
are building the whole thing.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.060
And we have expectations of
those roles, expectations

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:11.650
of the rights that
they have-- those

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:16.427
of a viewer, those of an editor,
those of an administrator.

00:12:16.427 --> 00:12:18.510
And of course, you have
the underlying resources--

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:21.670
BigQuery, Cloud
SQL, Cloud Spanner,

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:22.850
and so on and so forth.

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:29.280
All of that information
leads us to a separation

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:36.060
of concerns that then drives our
design into the technologies.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:38.310
You have users and groups.

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:41.910
Now, a user is
essentially an identity.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:43.650
It identifies who somebody is.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:49.350
Group is a collection of users
that also has an identity.

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:53.130
And we administer and
manage these in G Suite.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:56.480
There are other alternatives,
but I happen to prefer G Suite.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:00.080
I think it's a
great set of tools.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:02.090
We have roles and
we have resources.

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:04.100
Resources we just referred to.

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:06.650
It's all the stuff that we
want to use in Google Cloud

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:07.890
Platform.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.550
Roles are the glue that
brings it together.

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:16.040
Roles allow us to answer
questions such as,

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:19.400
can Bjergsen, who is a
member of our FX analyst

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:23.250
