00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:52.090
I've used it quite extensively
over the last year or so--

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:56.350
intuitive, easy to
use, and I loved it.

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:59.410
G Suite admin console
allows you to manipulate

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:01.720
security and users.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:06.490
Google Groups allows you
to manipulate groups.

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:09.530
Best practices is about
all we'll do here.

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:12.930
Fully managed users-- you can
create, suspend, and delete

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:17.870
users such that when something
becomes apparent to you

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:20.870
as an administrator or
to your administrator,

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:24.880
they can stop people accessing
your systems immediately.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:27.530
Password strength--
inside of G Suite,

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.140
you can both monitor and
enforce password strength,

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:32.240
and strong passwords are good.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.670
Two factor
authentication-- again,

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:38.090
you can monitor and
administer and enforce the use

00:14:38.090 --> 00:14:40.010
of two factor authentication.

00:14:40.010 --> 00:14:43.130
Now those things,
most likely, you

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:45.710
regard most of that as
common sense and so do I.

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:46.610
And that's great.

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:51.410
But actually, it pays down a
huge threat to your systems.

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:55.040
To be able to have an
elevated and increased level

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:58.460
of confidence that the
person using your systems

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:01.460
is who they say they are
and who you think they are

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:03.200
is just great.

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:06.080
It helps enormously
pay down a threat.

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:09.470
Somebody has to know something--
username and password--

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:12.500
as well as having
something-- the token

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:14.510
for two factor authentication.

00:15:14.510 --> 00:15:17.300
And our last best
practice is that we do not

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:23.960
allow open enrollment to groups
that we use for roles in GCP.

00:15:23.960 --> 00:15:26.480
We'll go through that a bit
more, but it makes sense.

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:30.170
If we are using groups to
somehow control access to GCP

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:32.540
and we allow anybody
to join any group,

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:37.540
then we've achieved
nothing whatsoever.

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:40.040
Technologies in Google
Cloud Platform--

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.920
Cloud Resource Manager
we'll go through first.

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:50.680
Cloud Resource Manager is a--

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:53.440
who here went to the earlier
session on Cloud Resource

00:15:53.440 --> 00:15:55.850
Manager?

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:57.800
Then I have a treat for you.

00:15:57.800 --> 00:16:00.950
Cloud resource manager
is a great idea.

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:03.530
It's a relatively
simple concept.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:05.810
And what it does is it
provides a mechanism

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:10.520
for grouping and hierarchically
organizing resources.

00:16:10.520 --> 00:16:12.950
Now, what I mean by
that is the following.

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:17.045
A resource is owned by a
single project with any access,

00:16:17.045 --> 00:16:19.910
but for resources
between projects

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:23.180
having to be
specifically set up.

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:25.910
Now what was introduced
earlier on in the day

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:30.000
is that you can organize
projects into folders.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.800
And you can nest
folders within folders.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:37.210
And then at the top
level, your organization

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:39.290
owns folders and projects.

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:43.680
And GCP sits over the
top of all of that.

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.010
And the powerful
concept there is

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:50.420
that once you have this in place
and you can decompose and model

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.640
your organization
within it, you can

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:56.780
start to operate above
the level of projects.

00:16:56.780 --> 00:17:00.620
You can apply things like
permissions to folders.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.080
You can view all
of the permissions

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:04.550
and all of the
projects that you've

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:08.010
got from your organization.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:13.430
Let's think of this in
the context of Example.

00:17:13.430 --> 00:17:16.550
As a G Suite customer,
Example already

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:21.380
has an organizational node
when it first gets into GCP.

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:25.069
Otherwise, you can simply ask
for one and you'll get one.

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:27.920
Underneath that, we
have FX, Compliance,

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:30.410
and Equities folders.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:33.170
Underneath those folders,
we create projects

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:36.470
for the Asian portfolio
and the US portfolio,

00:17:36.470 --> 00:17:38.960
and also for compliance,
because although they're just

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:40.850
looking over what
we're doing, they will

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:42.710
want to do their own analysis.

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:47.840
And underneath those, they
have their own resources.

00:17:47.840 --> 00:17:53.380
A question that came up a lot,
that comes up, but particularly

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:56.290
came up in the past a
lot, was how do people

00:17:56.290 --> 00:18:00.340
control the proliferation
of stuff in GCP?

00:18:00.340 --> 00:18:03.490
And usually, what they were
talking about is projects.

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:07.360
Because people had-- with
the best intentions in mind,

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:12.940
people knew that projects were
a good entity, a good trust

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:16.120
boundary to model
what they were doing.

00:18:16.120 --> 00:18:20.050
And yet they didn't want
that administration overhead

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:23.860
to scale with the number
of projects that they had.

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:26.890
Now, the answer to
that question now,

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:29.290
with Cloud Resource
Manager in place,

00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:32.140
is that you don't have anything
to worry about anymore.

00:18:32.140 --> 00:18:35.110
Embrace a proliferation
of projects

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:36.700
because it's your friend.

00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:40.600
It is the right place to have a
trust boundary about resources.

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:43.690
And now with folders
in place, you

00:18:43.690 --> 00:18:48.260
can begin to operate at
higher levels of abstraction

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:52.240
than simply projects so that the
administration overhead is not

00:18:52.240 --> 00:18:55.830
going to scale with projects.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:58.070
Second technology we'll
make a lot of use of

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.530
is Identity and
Access Management--

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:02.150
Cloud IAM.

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:06.770
Now what Cloud IAM does is
it's a way for administrators

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:13.570
to specify how identities
can interact with resources.

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:15.850
I'll explain the
concepts here together

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:18.670
with the example
at the same time.

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:21.135
First of all, you
have identities.

00:19:21.135 --> 00:19:21.760
You have users.

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:22.426
You have groups.

00:19:22.426 --> 00:19:23.890
You have service
accounts and we're

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:26.360
going to think about
Froggen at example.com

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:29.690
because he happens
to be my favorite.

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:32.990
And at the other end of the
spectrum, you have resources.

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:36.070
And in this case, we're
talking about BigQuery.

00:19:36.070 --> 00:19:38.470
Now, a user can be given a role.

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:40.720
Actually, a user can be
given multiple roles,

00:19:40.720 --> 00:19:43.540
but we'll think about this
specifically for BigQuery.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:45.490
So a user has a role.

00:19:45.490 --> 00:19:47.080
They can wear a hat.

00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:50.430
And in this case, we want that
to be BigQuery data viewer.

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:54.920
Froggen can view
data in BigQuery.

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:57.430
A role is composed
of permissions.

00:19:57.430 --> 00:20:00.070
It's a collection of
lower level permissions--

00:20:00.070 --> 00:20:02.860
BigQuery data sets
list, BigQuery data

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:06.310
sets get, all of the things
that you would intuitively

00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:10.510
know that a viewer of
BigQuery should have.

00:20:10.510 --> 00:20:13.960
A policy is the
collection of all roles

00:20:13.960 --> 00:20:17.020
that can apply to a resource--

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:20.740
data viewer, data owner, data
admin, all of those things

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:21.970
that happen.

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:26.740
And that chain of relationships
allows you to control how

00:20:26.740 --> 00:20:30.430
a user, how an identity
can have a role that's

00:20:30.430 --> 00:20:34.120
part of a policy that's
attached to a resource

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:39.260
and dictates how they can
interact with that resource.

00:20:39.260 --> 00:20:44.320
We-- when I started
writing this presentation,

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:46.420
we were actually going
to be interested in two

00:20:46.420 --> 00:20:47.310
different things.

00:20:47.310 --> 00:20:50.050
Another announcement
that came very recently

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:52.750
means that we're going to be
interested in three things.

00:20:52.750 --> 00:20:55.720
And it's good because it's
really additive to what

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:56.620
you want to do.

00:20:56.620 --> 00:21:00.310
The first thing we're interested
in are primitive roles--

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:02.560
owner, editor, viewer.

00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:05.890
These are broad,
umbrella types of role

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:09.610
that apply to the
whole platform.

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:14.530
If you are a viewer, you
can view all resources

00:21:14.530 --> 00:21:16.000
in a GCP project.

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:19.000
If you are an editor, you can
create and delete resources.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:20.897
If you're an owner,
you can create--

00:21:20.897 --> 00:21:21.730
you can do anything.

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:23.840
You can create and
delete projects.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:26.440
You can also apply
roles to people.

00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:27.990
So you can add people in.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:30.520
Those are primitive
roles and a lot of you

00:21:30.520 --> 00:21:33.340
will be familiar with those.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:37.330
IAM introduces
predefined roles, which

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:40.600
means that you have a lower
level, more granular way

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:45.420
of composing a set of roles,
such as data viewer, data

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:47.920
owner, data flow, developer.

00:21:47.920 --> 00:21:52.600
You can compose different roles
into what you want your user

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:53.870
to be able to do.

00:21:53.870 --> 00:21:57.460
We also now have custom roles,
which give you even more

00:21:57.460 --> 00:21:59.650
control over what goes on.

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:02.860
Custom roles simplify
things in a number of ways.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:04.960
You can set up--
we could create--

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:09.220
we, as we're building Example,
could create an analyst role.

00:22:09.220 --> 00:22:12.880
And we can create it from
taking underlying permissions.

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:16.810
And quite neatly, we can also
build it off of other roles.

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:20.490
So we could take an existing
role and we could say, yeah,

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:23.416
but I don't want to do that and
I don't want them to do that

00:22:23.416 --> 00:22:24.790
and I don't want
them to do that.

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:26.250
But all the rest of it is cool.

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:27.600
Let that go through.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:30.250
Custom roles are in
an open alpha now.

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:32.040
You can apply to
be part of that.

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:38.810
Let's apply this to Example.

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:41.670
FX analysts at
example.com we will make

00:22:41.670 --> 00:22:44.100
an editor of Foreign Exchange.

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:46.410
And I want to pause
there because I'm

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:51.240
using a primitive role here
just because it's easy to write.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:55.050
It's just-- I'm not implying
that this is a best practice.

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:58.740
This is just the term that
I'll use for the slides.

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.760
And we'll go into what is
the best practice later.

00:23:02.760 --> 00:23:05.190
Equities analyst,
editor of equities,

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:09.570
and compliance can view both
foreign exchange and equities

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:13.110
and is an editor of
its own folder, which

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.810
then means that any
projects that we

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:19.080
create underneath foreign
exchange, for example--

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:22.710
any more projects-- they
inherit the permissions

00:23:22.710 --> 00:23:27.180
from the folder from the
hierarchy that goes above them.

00:23:27.180 --> 00:23:30.300
One caveat to bear
in mind is that you

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:34.440
can get extra permissions at a
lower level in the hierarchy.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.170
You cannot take
permissions away.

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:41.160
So you need to bear that in mind
as you're building things out.

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:43.800
But what it does
mean is that as we're

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:46.530
creating lots and
lots of projects,

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:48.570
the administrative
overhead goes down.

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:51.430
You already have good
solid permissions in place.

00:23:54.760 --> 00:23:59.410
To this point, we have
organized our worlds

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:02.400
and we have established
trust within our worlds.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:08.500
And now as the old adage
goes, we'll trust but verify.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:12.730
Audit logging is
hyper important.

00:24:12.730 --> 00:24:17.320
I find that most people, they
understand resource manager

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:20.620
quite quickly and
they identify with IAM

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:23.410
even though the devil's
in the detail with IAM.

00:24:23.410 --> 00:24:27.610
And audit logging is sort of
something they also look at.

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:31.090
Audit logging is hyper
important because there's

00:24:31.090 --> 00:24:34.150
a couple of things that
you can probably guarantee.

00:24:34.150 --> 00:24:39.400
The one is that as we do the
best job we probably can,

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:43.170
it's probably going to be some
approximation of a good job.

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:45.470
And so you will
get things wrong.

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:47.830
The other thing is that
things change over time.

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:51.190
We're talking about a growing,
evolving enterprise and things

00:24:51.190 --> 00:24:52.700
change over time.

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:58.120
Audit logging is the physics
of what goes on in your system.

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:01.720
Who's interacting with
resources and who's

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:03.430
interacting with data?

00:25:03.430 --> 00:25:05.770
Who's creating things,
who's destroying

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:08.440
things, who's accessing things?

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:10.120
There's three
different ways that you

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:11.620
can look audit logging.

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:15.830
One of them is in the Activity
view from the console.

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:19.040
That is good and it is useful.

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:20.960
But the reason I dwell
over it a little bit

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:24.110
is I don't find it the most
useful way of doing it.

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:27.350
You can also look at it
through Stackdriver Logging.

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:31.400
That's what I like to do because
there, you can specify filters.

00:25:31.400 --> 00:25:33.350
You can search by date ranges.

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:36.200
You can create
alerts in Stackdriver

00:25:36.200 --> 00:25:39.770
and you can make
this truly useful.

00:25:39.770 --> 00:25:43.130
Because what I implied before
is that you should spend--

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:44.260
this is hyper important.

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:45.950
You should spend time on this.

00:25:45.950 --> 00:25:48.600
And yet, I'm trying to
reduce the amount of time

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:50.810
that you're spending
on administration.

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:53.180
And the way you
pay down that time

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:56.570
is by creating
appropriate filters,

00:25:56.570 --> 00:25:58.280
creating appropriate
alerts, because what

00:25:58.280 --> 00:26:01.760
you want to be working on
a forward looking basis

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:03.800
is exceptions
rather than looking

00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:05.510
at everything that goes on.

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:08.000
The final thing that you
can do with audit logs

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:12.500
is you can export them to Google
Cloud Storage or to BigQuery

00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:14.810
and you should
export them to both

00:26:14.810 --> 00:26:17.240
and you should keep
that data forever.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:20.650
Because eventually, you'll
want to investigate something.

00:26:20.650 --> 00:26:23.540
And certainly,
you'll want to learn.

00:26:23.540 --> 00:26:25.460
You're analysts.

00:26:25.460 --> 00:26:29.180
You'll want to analyze that data
to see what you can do better.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.440
Checkpoint-- Cloud
Resource Manager

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:37.750
defines a hierarchy,
allowing you to group

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.870
and operate on your projects
and resources effectively.

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:45.220
Cloud IAM allows you to
define roles and policies

00:26:45.220 --> 00:26:48.250
and apply them to different
levels of that hierarchy

00:26:48.250 --> 00:26:50.800
that you defined with
Cloud Resource Manager.

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:54.190
Cloud Audit Logging
allows you to introspect

00:26:54.190 --> 00:26:55.855
the physics of your systems.

00:27:00.600 --> 00:27:02.020
Let's go ahead and build it.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:07.530
Part of what I want to get
across in this presentation,

00:27:07.530 --> 00:27:10.860
because we do have to cover
a lot of ground in what we're

00:27:10.860 --> 00:27:15.150
talking about, is I want
to lodge those technologies

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:19.080
in your mind so that as you go
back and you're looking at GCP,

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:22.835
part of what you do is you
investigate these technologies.

00:27:22.835 --> 00:27:28.110
Part of a 20% project for
people that want to do well

00:27:28.110 --> 00:27:31.770
is to go off and learn more,
because we'll try and make

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:33.270
it as easy as possible.

00:27:33.270 --> 00:27:36.180
But the more you know,
the better you are.

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:38.496
So I want to, if I--

00:27:38.496 --> 00:27:42.390
I want to try and inspire you
to learn more about these things

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:45.300
as I learned more
about these things.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:46.200
I was in finance.

00:27:46.200 --> 00:27:49.200
I come from a classical
quant background

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:51.480
and I didn't naturally
know these things.

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:52.590
I joined Google.

00:27:52.590 --> 00:27:54.210
I learned about these things.

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:57.280
I became far better at my
job and far more useful.

00:28:01.745 --> 00:28:03.882
Oh, so we're going
to do two things.

00:28:03.882 --> 00:28:05.590
We're going to build
this thing and we're

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:08.630
going to mix up prerequisites
and steady state.

00:28:08.630 --> 00:28:12.800
And I will try and differentiate
between the two as we go along.

00:28:12.800 --> 00:28:18.790
First thing we do is our G Suite
administrator builds, creates,

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:22.090
fx-analysts@example.com,
equities-analysts,

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:22.930
and compliance.

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:24.970
And they do that
in Google Groups.

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:28.540
And they ensure that there's
not open enrollment to that.

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:30.310
And that looks pretty
straightforward

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:32.600
because it actually is
pretty straightforward.

00:28:32.600 --> 00:28:34.750
I built it out and it's
pretty straightforward

00:28:34.750 --> 00:28:37.660
to do that stuff-- one of
the obvious nice things

00:28:37.660 --> 00:28:38.800
about G Suite.

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:42.820
We can see those groups.

00:28:42.820 --> 00:28:44.530
We have three groups.

00:28:44.530 --> 00:28:47.440
Part of my secondment is
I'm acting as the G Suite

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:48.910
administrator for Example.

00:28:51.640 --> 00:28:57.000
G Suite admin console allows
us to create and manipulate

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:58.350
and monitor users.

00:28:58.350 --> 00:29:00.110
And again, it's very
easy to do that.

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:04.800
And we've created Bjergsen
and Froggen and Impact.

00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:11.310
And the next thing that we do is
we map those on to our groups.

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:14.280
And the one thing
that I'll mention here

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:15.990
is that the administrator
doesn't need

00:29:15.990 --> 00:29:17.760
to be a member of the group.

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:19.710
I happen to be the
administrator of the group

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:21.960
and you can see me as a
member of the group there.

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:23.940
I don't need to be a
member of the group.

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:25.980
Because if that was
true for all groups,

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:27.930
then the G Suite
administrator would somehow

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:30.439
be a super user of
all GCP projects

00:29:30.439 --> 00:29:31.980
and that doesn't
need to be the case.

00:29:35.130 --> 00:29:37.760
And now we move on
to GCP and we start

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:40.880
to look at our
prerequisites for GCP.

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:44.020
The first is that
we create folders.

00:29:44.020 --> 00:29:46.580
And our organizational
administrator

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:49.550
who, if you think about
our separation of concerns,

00:29:49.550 --> 00:29:51.800
doesn't have to be the
same person as the G Suite

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:53.350
administrator--

00:29:53.350 --> 00:29:56.180
can be for very small companies,
but you're pretty quickly

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:58.880
going to get to the point
where these two people will

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:01.760
be different or even
these two groups of people

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:02.990
will be different.

00:30:02.990 --> 00:30:07.640
Organization administrator
creates FX, Equities,

00:30:07.640 --> 00:30:09.970
and Compliance folders.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:12.320
And you can see the
organization view here.

00:30:12.320 --> 00:30:14.880
You can create
them in this view.

00:30:14.880 --> 00:30:19.130
You can also see everything
you've created in that view

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:20.750
and it's really neat.

00:30:20.750 --> 00:30:24.020
But it's not the only way
that you can do things on GCP.

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:26.840
Typically, there's always those
three ways that you can do it.

00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:28.520
You can do it
through the console,

00:30:28.520 --> 00:30:30.350
you can do it from
the command line,

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:32.450
or you can do it
through a REST API.

00:30:32.450 --> 00:30:34.850
And it brings up an
interesting question for us

00:30:34.850 --> 00:30:38.420
as to what's the best practice
for creating or manipulating

00:30:38.420 --> 00:30:39.470
these things.

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:41.660
Should you do it
from the console?

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:46.340
Should you do it essentially
as a coding exercise?

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:49.550
I regard this as a
grayer area because when

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:52.250
I talked to people about
their preferences here

00:30:52.250 --> 00:30:54.710
and then I factored
in my own preferences,

00:30:54.710 --> 00:30:57.950
there isn't a single school
of thought about this.

00:30:57.950 --> 00:31:02.044
I prefer to turn it
into a coding exercise

00:31:02.044 --> 00:31:04.460
because I come-- partly because
I come from an engineering

00:31:04.460 --> 00:31:09.440
background, partly because it's
reproducible, partly because I

00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:11.180
can stick it in
source code control,

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:16.190
and partly because I can
write code that tests my code.

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:17.630
And I prefer it that way.

00:31:17.630 --> 00:31:19.910
There's a
counter-argument to that

00:31:19.910 --> 00:31:23.330
is that creating and
modeling your organizations

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:27.410
is a relatively static process.

00:31:27.410 --> 00:31:28.700
You will do it.

00:31:28.700 --> 00:31:33.850
You will evolve it, but you
will evolve it somewhat slowly.

00:31:33.850 --> 00:31:37.360
And also, it interacts
with audit logging.

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:40.090
So everything that
happens in your system

00:31:40.090 --> 00:31:42.730
is caught in an audit log.

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:47.500
I happen to prefer making
it a coding exercise.

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:50.710
But perversely in
a way, I'm actually

00:31:50.710 --> 00:31:55.220
going to use the console going
through this presentation

00:31:55.220 --> 00:31:57.800
because I think it's prettier.

00:31:57.800 --> 00:31:59.900
The white and the
blue is nicer to look

00:31:59.900 --> 00:32:01.180
than the black and the white.

00:32:01.180 --> 00:32:08.070
So folders, prerequisites-- our
organizational administrator

00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:11.340
then maps our groups
onto our folders--

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:15.770
FX analyst as an editor
of FX, equities analysts

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:20.880
as an editor of equities, and
compliance as a reader of FX

00:32:20.880 --> 00:32:23.900
and equities and an
editor of compliance.

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:26.690
And I feel compelled to
just call out the fact

00:32:26.690 --> 00:32:29.430
that those primitive
roles are not necessarily

00:32:29.430 --> 00:32:30.950
the best practice.

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:33.979
And you can do that
through the same console.

00:32:33.979 --> 00:32:35.520
And it makes it very
easy because you

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:39.030
can select multiple
entities should you want to.

00:32:39.030 --> 00:32:41.010
So when you map
on compliance, you

00:32:41.010 --> 00:32:43.950
can just select FX
and Equities folders

00:32:43.950 --> 00:32:46.120
and apply the same role to both.

00:32:46.120 --> 00:32:48.020
So it makes it pretty
straightforward.

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:54.201
You can look at the
IAM view as well.

00:32:54.201 --> 00:32:56.200
So you can set it up from
the organization view.

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:59.990
You can look at the IAM page and
you can see what's happening.

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:02.500
Now that was prerequisites,
this is steady state.

00:33:02.500 --> 00:33:05.380
Our organizational
administrator creates

00:33:05.380 --> 00:33:07.750
our project, Asian Portfolio.

00:33:07.750 --> 00:33:13.000
Asian Portfolio inherits that
set of permissions from FX.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:16.480
That means that
fx-analysts@example.com can

00:33:16.480 --> 00:33:20.920
create, use, delete
resources on GCP,

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:26.480
whereas compliance@example.com
can only view those resources.

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:31.360
So our project creation, and
I said be happy and embrace

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:35.067
proliferation of projects,
becomes really straightforward.

00:33:39.050 --> 00:33:41.640
And there you can see
the organization view

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:44.060
with both projects
actually created.

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:51.260
Audit logging-- audit
logging, as I said before,

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:52.340
hyper important.

00:33:52.340 --> 00:33:56.270
Your organizational
administrator, or actually,

00:33:56.270 --> 00:33:59.870
somebody with a special
role, a private log viewer

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:01.490
role, because you
don't want audit

00:34:01.490 --> 00:34:03.710
logs being widely available.

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:06.380
You want that to
be a sort of either

00:34:06.380 --> 00:34:09.710
specialized or a filtered
experienced people.

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:11.179
You look at the audit logs.

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:13.190
You can see them there.

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:17.449
Stackdriver Logging UI--
you can use them there

00:34:17.449 --> 00:34:19.610
where you will spend the
majority of your time

00:34:19.610 --> 00:34:23.199
drilling in and filtering and
creating the right alerts.

00:34:23.199 --> 00:34:27.530
And what you will do is export
everything to BigQuery and GCS

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:29.199
and keep all of
that stuff forever.

00:34:35.710 --> 00:34:37.300
One more topic--

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:38.770
Access Control Lists.

00:34:38.770 --> 00:34:41.800
Access Control Lists,
ACLs, allow you fine

00:34:41.800 --> 00:34:45.429
grained control over
individual buckets and objects

00:34:45.429 --> 00:34:49.929
in GCS or over data sets
in table in BigQuery.

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:52.570
And the reason that that
is super useful to what

00:34:52.570 --> 00:34:56.080
you do going forward
is that you will

00:34:56.080 --> 00:34:58.750
have a situation such as
the following coming up

00:34:58.750 --> 00:34:59.440
quite quickly.

00:34:59.440 --> 00:35:03.430
Within Example, our
equities group is expanding

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:06.190
and they're interested
in Asian markets, which

00:35:06.190 --> 00:35:09.910
obviously means that they're
interested in exchange rates.

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:12.610
As they go from their home
country to the Asian markets,

00:35:12.610 --> 00:35:14.050
they want exchange rates.

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:16.930
Who better to give
them a fair exchange

00:35:16.930 --> 00:35:21.220
rate than their buddies
in the FX department?

00:35:21.220 --> 00:35:27.970
But if I apply a role such as
a storage viewer to equities,

00:35:27.970 --> 00:35:30.130
I'm allowing them to
look at all buckets.

00:35:30.130 --> 00:35:33.190
I'm giving them that
ubiquitously across FX

00:35:33.190 --> 00:35:34.500
and I don't want that.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:38.470
ACLs mean that I can
specify one bucket

00:35:38.470 --> 00:35:41.830
or I can specify one file
that I come up with daily.

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:47.210
So that's another useful
tool for people's arsenals.

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:50.240
Boom, we built out Example.

00:35:50.240 --> 00:35:52.640
And it didn't take
long and I hope

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:55.400
you see that the administrative
overhead that we've

00:35:55.400 --> 00:36:00.410
sort of incurred in our
enterprise is relatively low.

00:36:00.410 --> 00:36:05.090
And yet, we've decomposed
it in a really sensible way.

00:36:05.090 --> 00:36:06.560
It is secure.

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:07.940
It is easy to manage.

00:36:07.940 --> 00:36:11.510
We achieved our aims in
our build of Example.

00:36:11.510 --> 00:36:14.240
But we're not finished
because I went on and on

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:17.600
about it not necessarily
being a best practice

00:36:17.600 --> 00:36:19.060
to use primitive roles.

00:36:19.060 --> 00:36:26.270
And now I get to sort of
unburden myself about that one.

00:36:26.270 --> 00:36:30.140
Primitive roles are
broad-reaching things.

00:36:30.140 --> 00:36:33.140
And I wouldn't call them
legacy, but in some ways

00:36:33.140 --> 00:36:34.970
I regard them as
legacy concepts.

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:38.900
They can be useful, but
my personal preference

00:36:38.900 --> 00:36:40.370
is not to use them.

00:36:40.370 --> 00:36:46.070
I would always use predefined
or custom roles now.

00:36:46.070 --> 00:36:50.240
I'll give you two examples
that will sort of give voice

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:52.860
to my discomfort
with primitive roles.

00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:54.800
The first is that
if I was to make

00:36:54.800 --> 00:36:58.850
you an editor of a project,
then you can request a quota

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:00.270
increase as well.

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:03.760
And as an administrator
of the organization,

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:05.300
it's not totally what I want.

00:37:05.300 --> 00:37:07.340
I'll be able to
stand in front of it

00:37:07.340 --> 00:37:09.290
because I'll see
it in audit logging

00:37:09.290 --> 00:37:11.150
and I'll be able to
stop it, and I'll

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:12.710
have an alert created for it.

00:37:12.710 --> 00:37:14.210
But to be honest,
it would be better

00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:15.293
if it just didn't come up.

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:20.561
The other example
that I'll give you--

00:37:23.930 --> 00:37:26.105
I've completely forgotten.

00:37:26.105 --> 00:37:26.980
You give me a minute?

00:37:26.980 --> 00:37:28.625
It was a good example.

00:37:28.625 --> 00:37:29.666
AUDIENCE: It's 6 o'clock.

00:37:29.666 --> 00:37:30.707
AUDIENCE: It's 6 o'clock.

00:37:30.707 --> 00:37:32.296
CORRIE ELSTON: It's 6 o'clock?

00:37:32.296 --> 00:37:34.751
It's 6 o'clock, but this is
all really all I've done.

00:37:34.751 --> 00:37:36.500
I've manned the booths
and I've done this.

00:37:36.500 --> 00:37:38.370
It's not like I've
really grafted today.

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:42.870
Nope.

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.980
I'll come back to
it in a minute.

00:37:48.980 --> 00:37:51.420
So-- when I remember.

00:37:51.420 --> 00:37:56.040
So primitive roles
grant broad access,

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:58.340
but I don't think that
you should use them.

00:37:58.340 --> 00:37:59.942
Pre-defined roles
and custom roles

00:37:59.942 --> 00:38:01.650
are very interesting
because essentially,

00:38:01.650 --> 00:38:05.280
what we're talking about here is
a principle of least privilege.

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:07.830
And that's the best way
to view these things is

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:10.770
what you would like
to start off with is

00:38:10.770 --> 00:38:12.990
absolutely no permissions.

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:15.960
And then the safest thing
for you and I and everybody

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:18.780
is if you have only
those permissions that

00:38:18.780 --> 00:38:20.430
allow you to do your job.

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:22.320
It's good for you
because you can't make--

00:38:22.320 --> 00:38:23.880
I remember.

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:26.860
If I make you an
editor of the project,

00:38:26.860 --> 00:38:28.590
you can define firewall rules.

00:38:28.590 --> 00:38:30.645
And as an analyst, I
might not-- you know,

00:38:30.645 --> 00:38:32.310
it's something that
you might not want.

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:35.490
Again, stand in front of
it using audit logging.

00:38:35.490 --> 00:38:38.370
But again, it's better if
it actually didn't come up.

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:40.380
Pre-defined roles would
allow you to do that.

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:43.320
Pre-defined roles means
that we start from nothing

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:44.850
and we layer on roles.

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:47.010
I can say that you can
administer BigQuery.

00:38:47.010 --> 00:38:49.650
You can be a data flow
developer, all of the things

00:38:49.650 --> 00:38:53.700
that I know you'd want to
do as an analyst, those

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:55.346
and only those things.

00:38:55.346 --> 00:38:56.970
And if you ever think
of a great reason

00:38:56.970 --> 00:38:58.920
why you'd want to
do firewall rules,

00:38:58.920 --> 00:39:01.530
then we can have that discussion
at that point in time.

00:39:01.530 --> 00:39:04.410
It's a conscious
decision that we take.

00:39:04.410 --> 00:39:09.570
Custom roles are also useful
because you could address

00:39:09.570 --> 00:39:12.570
both of my concerns by
taking the editor role

00:39:12.570 --> 00:39:15.000
and removing the
quota administrator

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:17.910
and removing the ability
for networks and firewalls.

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:19.740
And you could build
it up that way.

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:23.650
You can also name things in
a way that makes more sense

00:39:23.650 --> 00:39:25.265
to your organization.

00:39:28.660 --> 00:39:32.890
Variation 2-- there
will be situations

00:39:32.890 --> 00:39:37.390
where you will want, possibly,
a greater degree of control

00:39:37.390 --> 00:39:39.280
over encryption.

00:39:39.280 --> 00:39:41.500
Now, Google gives you--

00:39:41.500 --> 00:39:43.060
and you've heard this--

00:39:43.060 --> 00:39:45.160
great encryption
by default. We'll

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:47.620
encrypt everything
at rest and we'll

00:39:47.620 --> 00:39:51.640
apply what Google believes to
be best practices over that.

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:52.570
We'll shard it.

00:39:52.570 --> 00:39:55.090
We'll put different
keys on the shards.

00:39:55.090 --> 00:39:57.460
We will organize those
keys into key chains

00:39:57.460 --> 00:40:00.670
and we will rotate
those keys frequently.

00:40:00.670 --> 00:40:04.150
So you couldn't-- the best you
can say about your encryption

00:40:04.150 --> 00:40:06.520
practices is that they're
as good as ours are.

00:40:06.520 --> 00:40:08.980
We regard ours as being
as good as there are

00:40:08.980 --> 00:40:11.080
and you can do as
good as we can.

00:40:11.080 --> 00:40:13.060
And I want to get
it across before we

00:40:13.060 --> 00:40:14.260
go into the variations.

00:40:14.260 --> 00:40:18.550
There may be some
regulatory requirements that

00:40:18.550 --> 00:40:22.600
mean that you want a different
level of interaction with keys,

00:40:22.600 --> 00:40:26.290
and there may be some aspect
of your company philosophy

00:40:26.290 --> 00:40:28.120
that puts you in
that way as well.

00:40:28.120 --> 00:40:30.890
And all power to you.

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:33.340
So cloud key
management service--

00:40:33.340 --> 00:40:37.420
what we're doing is a fully
managed key management service

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:40.750
that you can integrate
into your applications

00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:46.630
and you can create and maintain
hierarchies of keys on GCP.

00:40:46.630 --> 00:40:50.980
It interacts with cloud
IAM and audit logging.

00:40:50.980 --> 00:40:54.940
So you can have permissions such
that only certain people can

00:40:54.940 --> 00:40:58.660
create keys and they can be
different from the people that

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:00.700
can encrypt and decrypt data.

00:41:00.700 --> 00:41:02.620
And everything that
happens here will

00:41:02.620 --> 00:41:07.690
go into audit logging for
you to take a look at.

00:41:07.690 --> 00:41:11.470
Another option is
customer supplied keys.

00:41:11.470 --> 00:41:17.090
This is where you want the
key to be in your hands

00:41:17.090 --> 00:41:21.020
on your machines,
effectively, either because

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:24.190
of philosophy or because of
a regulatory requirement,

00:41:24.190 --> 00:41:27.380
or because you have an existing
key management infrastructure

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:28.925
that you want to keep using.

00:41:28.925 --> 00:41:33.560
Now, this works for Google
Compute Engine and Google Cloud

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:35.060
Storage.

00:41:35.060 --> 00:41:37.280
And it's not
completely transparent

00:41:37.280 --> 00:41:38.690
because there are
certain things.

00:41:38.690 --> 00:41:44.270
Like, you can't upload data
to a Cloud Storage bucket

00:41:44.270 --> 00:41:46.740
from the console and
yet use your own keys.

00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:48.680
So it's good.

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:52.970
It's not completely
seamless, but it

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:56.450
allows you to use your own
encryption approaches there.

00:42:00.070 --> 00:42:03.310
We request the key whenever
we need to use the key.

00:42:03.310 --> 00:42:05.170
We use it transiently.

00:42:05.170 --> 00:42:07.120
We never store that key.

00:42:07.120 --> 00:42:10.540
There is a cryptographic
hash of that key stored

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:13.040
that can't be used, obviously,
to reconstruct the key.

00:42:13.040 --> 00:42:14.920
So essentially,
we borrow that key

00:42:14.920 --> 00:42:17.290
from you for a minimal
length of time,

00:42:17.290 --> 00:42:20.320
do what you've asked us to
do, and then the key goes.

00:42:25.950 --> 00:42:29.940
And we came quite a long
way in that presentation.

00:42:29.940 --> 00:42:34.050
And what I wanted to achieve
was to sort of get you

00:42:34.050 --> 00:42:35.940
into the frame of
mind that I got

00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:38.610
into when thinking
about these things,

00:42:38.610 --> 00:42:41.640
to introduce the technologies
that I think you should take

00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:46.670
away and learn more about
and become far more expert

00:42:46.670 --> 00:42:49.500
in because it's going
to help you enormously.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:52.080
And a couple of those things
are recent announcements.

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:55.080
Custom roles are coming
out, and folders,

00:42:55.080 --> 00:43:02.990
which really is a really prime
idea, was announced today.

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:07.170
Takeaways-- these set of
takeaways come best practices.

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:11.850
That's the sort of culmination
of everything that we've done.

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:15.630
Fully managed users, strong
passwords, two factor

00:43:15.630 --> 00:43:20.970
authentication, full stop, if I
had a microphone, because those

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:23.280
are things that you want to do.

00:43:23.280 --> 00:43:25.530
No open enrollment to groups--

00:43:25.530 --> 00:43:28.620
it just makes
perfectly good sense.

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:32.490
You can use the console
or the command line API

00:43:32.490 --> 00:43:38.040
for administering
organizations and IAM.

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:42.690
I prefer using the command
line because I can write

00:43:42.690 --> 00:43:45.060
unit tests for what's going on.

00:43:45.060 --> 00:43:48.990
And if you go towards using
predefined roles and custom

00:43:48.990 --> 00:43:51.660
roles, then being
able to unit test

00:43:51.660 --> 00:43:54.645
those things is very useful
to you going forward.

00:43:57.760 --> 00:44:02.050
Use an organization to
centralize project and resource

00:44:02.050 --> 00:44:04.090
administration.

00:44:04.090 --> 00:44:07.090
Use folders to model
your organization,

00:44:07.090 --> 00:44:12.310
grouping like projects together
by team or functional area.

00:44:12.310 --> 00:44:17.440
Use nested folders to model
aspects of your organization

00:44:17.440 --> 00:44:19.720
development versus production.

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:25.000
Something we didn't touch on
is a separation of concerns.

00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:28.870
Folders can also help to model
a separation of concerns.

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:33.610
It's not hyper important
to a lot of what

00:44:33.610 --> 00:44:37.310
I've done in data analysis,
but it is hyper important

00:44:37.310 --> 00:44:37.990
generally.

00:44:37.990 --> 00:44:40.750
You may want your developers
to be a different set of people

00:44:40.750 --> 00:44:41.620
entirely.

00:44:41.620 --> 00:44:43.780
In fact, regulations
might mean you absolutely

00:44:43.780 --> 00:44:46.390
have to have a different set
of people entirely looking

00:44:46.390 --> 00:44:48.910
at development and production.

00:44:48.910 --> 00:44:52.390
Be happy with the
proliferation of projects.

00:44:52.390 --> 00:44:54.610
It's a good thing for you.

00:44:54.610 --> 00:44:57.760
So audit logs--
export audit logs

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:01.810
to BigQuery and
Google Cloud Storage

00:45:01.810 --> 00:45:04.270
and keep them indefinitely.

00:45:04.270 --> 00:45:05.890
Monitor your audit logs.

00:45:05.890 --> 00:45:09.700
Create alerts where appropriate
and use this as feedback

00:45:09.700 --> 00:45:13.420
for refactoring
folders or roles.

00:45:13.420 --> 00:45:16.510
You can use primitive
roles, predefined roles,

00:45:16.510 --> 00:45:22.660
or custom roles to control
access effectively.

00:45:22.660 --> 00:45:26.920
You should use predefined
roles or custom roles.

00:45:26.920 --> 00:45:31.390
Encryption can be refined with
cloud key management, cloud

00:45:31.390 --> 00:45:38.050
KMS, or client supplied keys
for particular point use cases.

00:45:38.050 --> 00:45:40.120
Don't make that your go-to.

00:45:40.120 --> 00:45:44.490
Make that somewhere where you're
sort of pushed to on demand.

00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:51.870
Honorable mentions from today
go to the identity aware proxy

00:45:51.870 --> 00:45:55.950
that was released and to data
loss prevention because that's

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:57.420
going to be really useful.

00:45:57.420 --> 00:45:59.220
And I didn't
include it because I

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:02.740
didn't think it was totally core
to what we're talking about,

00:46:02.740 --> 00:46:05.730
but data prep is
really cool as well.

00:46:05.730 --> 00:46:08.926
For your analysts that
are out there working--

00:46:08.926 --> 00:46:12.300
and my day job is as an
analyst sort of working--

00:46:12.300 --> 00:46:13.810
super important.

00:46:13.810 --> 00:46:15.570
It's not really
management or security,

00:46:15.570 --> 00:46:18.840
but it is as cool
as cool can be.

00:46:18.840 --> 00:46:22.440
And with that, it's been a
pleasure and a privilege.

00:46:22.440 --> 00:46:24.300
Thank you very
much for your time.

00:46:24.300 --> 00:46:27.350
